module gunbc.fleet.runtime_identity_match

// WHO IS ON THE NETWORK (cut 6, S10 dry half, of gunbc.plans.physical_machine_boot_program).
//
// A unit has been network-booted. Something now has to bind "the machine that answered on the network" to
// "the unit the attempt was made for", because every later step -- enrolling it, installing on it, letting
// the control plane act through it -- acts on the ANSWER of that binding. This module is the binding's
// EVIDENCE HALF: the axes observable before the boot from the firmware side, the same axes reported by the
// running OS afterwards, and a total match fn whose three verdicts mean three different things.
//
// WHY THE THREE VERDICTS ARE NOT TWO, which is the whole cut:
// Unprovable is not Mismatch. A unit whose BMC never answered the serial query, or whose OS image does not
// ship the tool that reads a model string, has told us NOTHING -- it has not told us it is a different
// machine. Reporting that as a mismatch condemns a unit for its BMC's silence, and in this fleet BMC
// silence is routine (S2's silent-BMC defect, and the rows where a controller rejects a query outright).
// So: a divergence between two readings is Mismatch; a missing reading on either side is Unprovable; only
// agreement across everything that had to be checked is Match. The refusal is preserved as itself rather
// than collapsed into absence, because "the controller said no" and "nobody asked" lead to different human
// actions.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT:
// - NO IDENTITY DATA HERE. No row in this module carries a serial, a model, or an address: fixtures live in
//   witness fns only. A module that shipped a unit's real identity would be a second, unauditable register
//   of who the fleet's machines are, next to the intake subject that already owns that.
// - NO PER-HOST BRANCHING. Nothing here is keyed on a machine name, and no axis policy varies by unit. The
//   unit is CARRIED (as data, on both sides, and on the verdict as a PAIR) and never matched on: the proof
//   is entirely in the axes. Carrying both keys on the verdict is what keeps the carry honest -- a verdict
//   that named only one of them would be asserting a pairing it never tested.
// - NO TRANSPORT IN THE DECISION. Nothing that decides a verdict is a transport fact: no command line, no
//   tool, no protocol verb appears in any type or fn here. The post-boot side does carry WHICH KIND OF
//   CHANNEL reported -- as a semantic axis, in one place -- because a channel determines which axes are
//   answerable at all, and an axis is the only shape where a report and its channel cannot contradict each
//   other. Deciding is done on the readings; the channel is context that is stated, never inferred.

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr }
import std.optional { Optional, Present, Absent }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import std.dissolution { DissolutionCondition, DissolutionStatus, BoundDissolution, DeclarationAppears, dissolution_status }
import gunbc.machine_intake.subject { UnitKey }
import extdeps.network.mac { Eui48Address, mac_is_permanent_interface_identity, render_mac_address }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE AXES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The identity axes a boot attempt can be proven on. Closed, because the verdict must be accountable to
// a fixed list: a verdict that could omit an axis silently would be a verdict about whatever happened to be
// asked. An additional axis (a TPM attestation quote, say) adds an arm here, a union below, a field on
// each side, and a row in the axis table -- and the match fn's totals stay total because the verdict is
// a fold over this union rather than a hand-written chain.
type RuntimeIdentityAxis
  = BoardSerialAxis
  | BiosVersionAxis
  | ModelAxis
  | NicSetAxis

// How much an axis carries, as a policy the table can state rather than a bool the fold guesses with.
//
// DECISIVE axes must be read on BOTH sides and must agree, or there is no binding. CORROBORATING axes are
// checked only for divergence: a corroborating axis that goes unanswered does not void the binding, and a
// corroborating axis that CONTRADICTS does. That asymmetry is the point -- treating a corroborating gap as
// fatal would make every unit with a mute BMC unprovable forever, and treating a corroborating divergence
// as harmless would let a wrong machine through on the strength of a serial.
type AxisWeight
  = DecisiveAxis
  | CorroboratingAxis

// ONE ROW PER AXIS: what it weighs, how it is compared, and who says so.
type RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  weight: AxisWeight
  // TWO homes, both required, because a row makes TWO claims and a single citation can own only one of
  // them -- DESIGN section 3b calls that a scope mismatch, and it is worse than no citation because it
  // looks audited. `reading_authority` owns the FIELD this axis reads (its spelling, and that it can be
  // absent); `binding_authority` owns the stronger claim, that this field can bind two sightings of a
  // machine into one unit. A row citing only the reading home would smuggle in an identity claim no home
  // has granted -- the fleet's own position on the board-serial field is that it is a corroborating pointer
  // and not a proof, which is exactly why the serial row must also name the home that mints a unit key from
  // it. A future requirement to cite a third kind of authority adds a field here and a value per row; the
  // rows are data, so no fn changes.
  reading_authority: DeclarationRef
  // OPTIONAL, AND ONLY BECAUSE TWO OF THE FOUR AXES GENUINELY HAVE NO BINDING HOME. A binding authority
  // is the home that grants the claim "two sightings of this field are sightings of ONE unit". Only the
  // decisive axes make that claim, and only the decisive axes may bind alone; a corroborating axis can
  // report that two readings agree without any home having granted it the power to conclude they are the
  // same machine. Before this was optional, the corroborating rows each carried a second citation for a
  // power they do not exercise -- a digest over manifest ENTRIES cited as licensing the comparison of a
  // VERSION STRING, which is the scope mismatch DESIGN section 3b names, and it was made necessary by the
  // shape rather than by the facts. `Absent` now states the true position: this axis binds nothing, and a
  // Match that rests on it rests on the decisive axes. Adding a fifth axis that CAN bind means naming a
  // second home that actually grants it -- and if no such home exists, the honest answer is `Absent`, and
  // the axis is corroborating.
  binding_authority: DeclarationRef?
}

// THE AXIS TABLE, as authored evidence. Every row cites TWO declarations, each of which must (a) exist,
// (b) resolve uniquely in the corpus, and (c) own the claim the row leans on it for -- a citation that
// resolves but owns only part of the claim is a scope mismatch, worse than no citation because it looks
// audited (DESIGN section 3b). That is why the census test --
// `test_axis_table_citations_resolve_against_the_declaration_census` -- reads the citation pairs OUT OF
// THESE ROWS instead of restating them: a restated list can keep passing over a row whose citation was
// renamed or invented, and a citation that resolves to nothing is worse than none, because it reads as
// audited.
//
// What each home is asked to own, and why that one:
// - BoardSerial reads BoardSerialObservation (the field, its source, and that it may be absent) and names
//   bind_unit_key as its binding home -- WITH ITS SCOPE STATED, because the citation is easy to over-read
//   (merry-fox-82's side-chat verdict on head d5a72591). `bind_unit_key` is the fn that mints a UnitKey from a serial and refuses when
//   a serial is absent, duplicated across units, or contradictory; THIS MODULE DOES NOT RUN THOSE CHECKS and
//   cannot, because it sees two readings and no ledger of other units. What it borrows is the home's
//   *concept* -- a serial is a unit identifier only under that home's authority -- not the home's whole
//   decision procedure. The fold's serial comparison is therefore a text agreement under a named home, and
//   an operator still needs the ledger to learn the serial is not duplicated: this verdict is one input to
//   a binding, never the binding itself. A future requirement to rule out a duplicated serial here adds a
//   row-level obligation to pass `other_units` in, not a quieter comment about the citation.
// - BiosVersion reads H11sslBmcReportedIdentity, the fleet's own carrier of a BIOS version a unit reports.
//   It carries no binding home at all: it cites no digest, because a digest over firmware manifest ENTRIES
//   does not license comparing a VERSION STRING, and an honest `Absent` beats a citation that makes the row
//   look audited against a home owning a different fact. The axis stays Corroborating because a version
//   string can be shared by every unit on a batch; only the decisive axes can bind alone.
// - Model reads FruIdentityFields (board_product, board_part_number, board_serial as a controller decodes
//   them) and carries NO binding home: a model string names a PRODUCT, not a machine, and every unit of a
//   batch reports the same one. RecordedFruObservation was cited here as a second home, which is a real
//   authority over a claim this axis never made -- that is the shape DESIGN section 2 calls re-invention
//   and section 3b calls unauditable. `Absent` is the row saying so. It is a weaker claim than the
//   serial's key, which is why this row is Corroborating and what it can ever claim is that two strings
//   agree.
// - NicSet reads Eui48Address and binds through mac_is_permanent_interface_identity, the predicate that
//   owns when an address is a permanent interface identity at all -- which now includes excluding the
//   all-zero NULL address, which the flags alone accept (merry-fox-82's side-chat verdict on d5a72591). A shared address can only bind two
//   sightings if an address can name an interface, so the row's Decisive weight is borrowed from that
//   predicate and nothing else. WHAT THE ROW DOES NOT CLAIM: both sides are read from a *current* address
//   source, not from a permanent-address source (a Linux `addr_assign_type`/`perm_addr` reading, which has
//   no home in this corpus today). So the decisive claim is exactly "the addresses each side currently
//   reports as permanent agree on at least one entry", and the fold SCREENS rather than trusts: an address
//   that a caller cannot show is permanent never reaches the comparison. A future requirement to key on a
//   true permanent-address reading adds a row whose reading_authority names the home that publishes
//   addr_assign_type, and until such a home exists it must name `Absent` rather than borrow a neighbour's.
//
// A future requirement to make a fourth axis decisive adds a row with a different weight and TWO homes that
// own those two claims; no fn here changes, which is what makes the policy reviewable without reading the
// fold. Whether a home really OWNS its claim is a human judgment no fn can audit, so it is argued here and
// cited as data; what the witness can and does check mechanically is uniqueness and that no row satisfies
// the two-home requirement by naming one declaration twice.
data runtime_identity_axis_rows: List<RuntimeIdentityAxisRow> = [
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: BoardSerialAxis,
    weight: DecisiveAxis,
    reading_authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.subject", decl_name: "BoardSerialObservation"),
    binding_authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.subject", decl_name: "bind_unit_key"),
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: BiosVersionAxis,
    weight: CorroboratingAxis,
    // The reading home is the fleet's own carrier of a BIOS version a unit REPORTS: the record literally
    // has a `bios_version` field, and that is the whole of what this axis reads. It used to cite
    // subject.FirmwareManifest, which is a set of firmware ENTRIES plus content DIGESTS -- a digest over
    // manifest entries does not license comparing a version string, and the citation made the axis look
    // audited against a home that owns a different fact (merry-fox-82's side-chat verdict on d5a72591).
    reading_authority: decl_ref(
      module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_first_contact",
      decl_name: "H11sslBmcReportedIdentity",
    ),
    // No binding home, because none is needed and none exists: two units can ship the same BIOS version,
    // which is exactly why this axis is Corroborating. A digest over firmware entries is not a licence to
    // conclude that two reported version strings mean one machine.
    binding_authority: Absent,
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: ModelAxis,
    weight: CorroboratingAxis,
    reading_authority: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtjade1_access_observation", decl_name: "FruIdentityFields"),
    // No binding home. A model string identifies a PRODUCT, not a machine: every unit of a batch reports
    // the same one, so the strongest honest claim this axis makes is that two reported strings agree.
    // RecordedFruObservation (the sealed capture carrier) was cited here as the binding home, which is a
    // real authority over a claim this axis does not make.
    binding_authority: Absent,
  },
  RuntimeIdentityAxisRow {
    axis: NicSetAxis,
    weight: DecisiveAxis,
    reading_authority: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.network.mac", decl_name: "Eui48Address"),
    binding_authority: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.network.mac", decl_name: "mac_is_permanent_interface_identity"),
  },
]


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EACH AXIS, AS A STANDING -- one union each, with the three states as ARMS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a controller's or an OS's "no" is on an identity axis. Closed, because the remedy differs: a query
// the controller does not implement is a row to pin, an authentication failure is an operator action, and
// an unreadable reply is a defect in the reader. A string cannot split those.
type RuntimeIdentityRefusal
  = QueryUnsupported { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | AuthenticationFailed { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | ReplyUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }

// THE ONE AXIS STANDING, shared by every axis. Four separate sums used to live here -- serial, firmware
// version, model, address set -- each the same three states with a different payload name, and each with its
// own absence projection beside siblings that answer the same question. That is one concept written four
// times (DESIGN section 2: model a concept once and derive every use; its Int8..UInt128 example is exactly
// this, one axis rather than ten types), and it made a fifth axis a fifth sum plus a fifth projection
// instead of a row. What varies between axes is only the PAYLOAD TYPE, so that is what the parameter carries.
//
// The three states are ARMS, not a bool beside a value: a record with `read: Optional<T>` next to
// `refused: Bool` can name a reading AND a refusal at once, or neither, and the wrapper then disagrees with
// itself about whether anything was ever read. Here the states are mutually exclusive by construction.
//
// WHAT A FIFTH AXIS COSTS: an arm on `RuntimeIdentityAxis`, a field on each side record, and a row in the
// table. NOT a union and NOT an absence fn. If a genuinely different state ever appears (a reading that is
// stale rather than absent, say), it is a new arm here plus a new `AxisExamination` arm -- and every fold
// over them stops compiling until it handles it, which is the price of getting the fourth axis free today.
type AxisStanding<T>
  = AxisWasRead { value: T }
  | AxisStayedSilent { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | AxisWasRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }

// WHAT ONE AXIS SAYS ABOUT A PAIR OF STANDINGS -- the single answer the fold consumes. Three fns used to
// hand-match coproducts into bools (`both_sides_read`, `axis_is_decisive`, `same_runtime_identity_axis`) and
// the fold branched on those bools: each had already thrown away WHICH ARM it came from, a new arm would
// have been silently false, and a reader could not see the weight behind a decision. Here the evidence
// travels with the verdict about it, the weight is CARRIED rather than flattened to "is decisive", and a new
// state makes every consumer of this sum stop compiling until it handles the new arm.
//
// TWO ARMS, NOT THREE, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT. There used to be a third, `AxisIncomparable`, justified as
// "unreachable but total": `axis_pair` decided whether both sides had been read and a SEPARATE `side_gap`
// decided which side had not, so their leftovers had nowhere to go and landed in an arm every fold returned
// `[]` for -- which the fold read as "no divergence, no gap", i.e. `Match`. An unreachable arm that is
// dropped silently is not a safety net, it is the escape hatch DESIGN section 5 rejects by name, and the
// comment claiming it "surfaces to an operator" was false. The fix is not to make that arm fatal but to
// delete its constructor: `standing_report` below now answers both questions from ONE match over the two
// standings, so a state where neither arm applies cannot be built in the first place (correctness by
// construction, not validation).
type AxisExamination
  = AxisCompared { axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis, pair: AxisReadingPair, weight: AxisWeight }
  | AxisUnanswered { axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis, gaps: List<RuntimeIdentityGap>, weight: AxisWeight }

// WHICH SIDE FAILED TO ANSWER, decided by the same single match that decides whether a pair exists. The two
// questions -- "is there a pair to compare?" and "if not, who stayed quiet and why?" -- are ONE fact about
// the same two standings, and deriving them twice is what produced the arm with no honest meaning above.
//
// FOUR ARMS, AND THE FOURTH EXISTS BECAUSE THREE WERE NOT ENOUGH. The first version of this sum had
// `PreQuiet`/`PostQuiet` only, and claimed the firmware side "wins" when both were quiet. That was an
// argument about the ORDER in which two absences should be REPORTED, used as an argument for DELETING one:
// with a silent BMC and a booted OS that refused the read, the verdict came out `Unprovable` for one
// firmware `Unread` and the refusal vanished -- while a refusal names a different next human than a silence
// does, and the fleet's own history (BMCs that reject vendor commands) is exactly the case an operator
// needs to see. So BOTH absences are carried when both sides failed, and the firmware side is first in the
// list, which is all the old "wins" ever justified.
//
// WHY THE SILENT/REFUSED MAPPING APPEARS ONCE PER SIDE. A gap that cannot name which side stayed quiet
// cannot be routed -- the firmware row and the OS row are different places to go and different humans -- so
// the side must be spoken by the match itself rather than recovered from a value afterwards.
type AxisReport<T>
  = BothAnswered { pre_value: T, post_value: T }
  | PreQuiet { absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence }
  | PostQuiet { absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence }
  | BothQuiet { pre_absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence, post_absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence }

fn standing_report<T>(pre: AxisStanding<T>, post: AxisStanding<T>) -> AxisReport<T> {
  match pre {
    AxisWasRead { value: pv } => match post {
      AxisWasRead { value: ov } => BothAnswered { pre_value: pv, post_value: ov }
      AxisStayedSilent { reason: r } => PostQuiet { absence: Unread { reason: r } }
      AxisWasRefused { refusal: f } => PostQuiet { absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
    }
    AxisStayedSilent { reason: r } => match post {
      AxisWasRead { value: _ } => PreQuiet { absence: Unread { reason: r } }
      AxisStayedSilent { reason: r2 } =>
        BothQuiet { pre_absence: Unread { reason: r }, post_absence: Unread { reason: r2 } }
      AxisWasRefused { refusal: f } =>
        BothQuiet { pre_absence: Unread { reason: r }, post_absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
    }
    AxisWasRefused { refusal: f } => match post {
      AxisWasRead { value: _ } => PreQuiet { absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f } }
      AxisStayedSilent { reason: r2 } =>
        BothQuiet { pre_absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f }, post_absence: Unread { reason: r2 } }
      AxisWasRefused { refusal: f2 } =>
        BothQuiet { pre_absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f }, post_absence: ControllerRefused { refusal: f2 } }
    }
  }
}

// THE GAPS ONE REPORT LEAVES, in report order: firmware side first, then the booted-OS side.
fn gaps_of_report<T>(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, report: AxisReport<T>) -> List<RuntimeIdentityGap> {
  match report {
    BothAnswered { pre_value: _, post_value: _ } => []
    PreQuiet { absence: a } => [RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PreBootFirmwareSide, absence: a }]
    PostQuiet { absence: a } => [RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PostBootOsSide, absence: a }]
    BothQuiet { pre_absence: pa, post_absence: oa } => [
      RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PreBootFirmwareSide, absence: pa },
      RuntimeIdentityGap { axis: row.axis, side: PostBootOsSide, absence: oa },
    ]
  }
}

// WHICH AXIS AN EXAMINATION IS ABOUT. A projection over BOTH arms, deliberately not a pair of bool probes:
// the witness uses it to count examinations per axis, which is how "no axis was quietly dropped" is asserted
// now that the third arm is gone -- with two arms, an axis that produced nothing would previously have been
// invisible in exactly the way the deleted arm made invisible.
fn examination_axis(e: AxisExamination) -> RuntimeIdentityAxis {
  match e {
    AxisCompared { axis: a, pair: _, weight: _ } => a
    AxisUnanswered { axis: a, gaps: _, weight: _ } => a
  }
}

// THE ONE MATCH PER AXIS, and the only place an AxisExamination is built. Both arms cover every combination
// of the two standings, so the fold can never be handed a reading it has no policy for.
fn examine_axis<T>(
  row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow,
  pre: AxisStanding<T>,
  post: AxisStanding<T>,
  build: fn(T, T) -> AxisReadingPair,
) -> AxisExamination {
  let report = standing_report(pre: pre, post: post)
  match report {
    BothAnswered { pre_value: pv, post_value: ov } =>
      AxisCompared { axis: row.axis, pair: build(pv, ov), weight: row.weight }
    PreQuiet { absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
    PostQuiet { absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
    BothQuiet { pre_absence: _, post_absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
  }
}

// WHY THIS IS ITS OWN FN AND NOT THREE COPIES OF ONE LITERAL. `examination_axis` cannot bind the report to
// a name and still match on it exhaustively, so the three quiet arms call this. Writing the AxisUnanswered
// literal out three times is the DESIGN section 3 fork this avoids: a fifth AxisReport arm would then have
// to be noticed in three places instead of one.
fn unanswered<T>(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, report: AxisReport<T>) -> AxisExamination {
  AxisUnanswered { axis: row.axis, gaps: gaps_of_report(row: row, report: report), weight: row.weight }
}
// What an axis did not answer, and WHETHER it stayed quiet or was refused. The absence is a SUM, not a
// string: `Unread` (nobody got an answer) and `ControllerRefused` (an answer of "no" came back, with its
// reason) lead to different human actions, and once both are flattened into one `NonEmptyStr` the closed
// refusal sum cannot be recovered from the gap -- a declared variant that is then erased is the anemic
// modeling DESIGN section 2 forbids. The witness
// `test_refusal_is_unprovable_and_preserves_its_kind` is what keeps this honest.
// THE THIRD ARM IS NOT A SYNONYM FOR `Unread`. `Unread` means the side gave no answer at all; this arm
// means the side ANSWERED and the answer was not evidence -- it reported a list of addresses, and not one
// of them was a permanent interface identity (all-null, every entry locally administered or group, or the
// list simply empty). Folding that into `Unread` would tell an operator "nobody answered", which is the
// wrong diagnosis and the wrong next human; folding it into a divergence would report two machines that
// never disagreed as disagreeing (DESIGN section 5, a fabricated output). Only `reported_count` is carried:
// the eligible count is 0 by definition here -- that is what the arm's NAME asserts -- and a field that can
// hold exactly one value is noise beside a real one. The count is what makes the gap actionable: "reported
// 4, none eligible" is a probe bug, "reported 0" is a unit problem, and those are different tickets. The
// addresses themselves are NOT carried, because this module must not become a storable register of who the
// fleet's machines are (header), and a gap is not the place to start.
type RuntimeIdentityAbsence
  = Unread { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ControllerRefused { refusal: RuntimeIdentityRefusal }
  | NoEligibleEvidence { reported_count: Int }

// Which axis did not answer, on which side, and whether it was silence or a refusal.
type RuntimeIdentityGap {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  side: IdentitySide
  absence: RuntimeIdentityAbsence
}

// WHICH SIDE of the boot a reading comes from. Named, so an Unprovable can say whether the firmware side
// or the running-OS side is the one that stayed quiet -- the operator's next action differs completely.
type IdentitySide
  = PreBootFirmwareSide
  | PostBootOsSide

// A divergence, carrying BOTH readings as the pair that was actually compared -- not as two renderings.
// The pair is what makes a Mismatch actionable ("the BMC says X and the OS says Y" is a diagnosis where
// "the serials differ" is an accusation), and carrying the pair rather than a rendering is what keeps this
// total: the address axis can diverge because one side reported NO addresses at all, and a rendering of that
// side into a `NonEmptyStr` would have to be fabricated -- either a refinement cast that asserts something
// unproven, or a sentinel string standing in for absence. Both are forbidden (DESIGN section 5: no
// fabricated output; absence uses the language's absence type). So the divergence stores what the sides
// SAID. A report renders an address side into a LIST of `render_mac_address` strings, where "I was told
// nothing" is a length rather than a lie; the consumer decides what absence looks like to its reader. Adding an axis that reports something other than text or
// addresses adds an arm to that pair and to those two fns -- the compiler asks, rather than a cast
// silently agreeing to produce a string of whatever length it gets.
type RuntimeIdentityDivergence {
  axis: RuntimeIdentityAxis
  readings: AxisReadingPair
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TWO SIDES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// THE PRE-BOOT STANDING: what the firmware side said before the boot, per axis. Every axis is present as a
// field, and each field is itself a three-way union, so there is no way to state a pre-boot standing that
// quietly omits an axis -- and no way to claim a value while admitting nothing was read.
//
// The unit is CARRIED here and on the other side, and the match fn never compares the two keys (both keys
// are handed to the verdict unchanged, so nothing is lost and nothing is inferred about their relation):
// this is a
// proof about the machine's own reportings, not a check that two labels were spelled the same.
type PreBootIdentityStanding {
  unit: UnitKey
  board_serial: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  bios_version: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  model: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  nics: AxisStanding<List<Eui48Address>>
}

// THE POST-BOOT REPORT: the same four axes, as the running OS reports them.
type PostBootIdentityReport {
  unit: UnitKey
  reported_over: PostBootReportChannel
  board_serial: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  bios_version: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  model: AxisStanding<NonEmptyStr>
  nics: AxisStanding<List<Eui48Address>>
}

// HOW the OS came to report. A semantic axis rather than a transport detail, because it changes what the
// report can cover: a machine reachable only over a serial console has no session that can read every axis,
// and a gap on such a unit is expected rather than suspicious. It stays an axis so the reading and the
// channel cannot disagree about themselves -- a report cannot claim a full axis set over a channel that
// cannot carry one, because there is exactly one place both are stated.
type PostBootReportChannel
  = NetworkSessionChannel
  | SerialConsoleChannel

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE VERDICT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Three verdicts, closed, each carrying what makes it true.
//
// Match carries the axes it actually checked and passed -- not a bare "true", so a Match can be audited
// against the table afterwards. Mismatch carries both readings for every axis that diverged. Unprovable
// carries the named gaps that prevented a verdict, which is what makes "we do not know" a reportable state
// rather than a silent false.
// Every verdict carries BOTH keys, because a verdict that names one unit is a claim about a binding, and
// the fold never checked the pairing: built from `pre.unit` alone, a pre-boot standing for unit A and a
// post-boot report for unit B whose axes happened to agree returned `Match { unit: A }` -- an identity
// asserted from one of its two inputs, which is a fabricated binding (DESIGN section 5: no fabricated
// default). The fix is not to compare the keys (the ruling below forbids that) and not to add a fourth
// verdict for a mismatched pair (that would be branching on a name, and it would say "these are different
// machines" from the one piece of evidence that can never say it). It is for the verdict to stop pretending
// it bound a single unit: it reports the pair it related, and `Match { pre_unit: A, post_unit: B }` is an
// operator's cue that the pairing was never established, rather than a guarantee that it was.
//
// Both are `UnitKey`, the branded key the intake subject owns and that the serial row cites as its binding
// authority -- not a bare string re-minted here (DESIGN section 2: net concepts must not grow by
// re-invention). An unmatched pair is representable precisely BECAUSE the type is the same on both sides:
// the module cannot tell an honest pair from a mis-paired one, so it refuses to decide, and says both names.
type RuntimeIdentityVerdict
  = Match { axes_matched: List<RuntimeIdentityAxis>, pre_unit: UnitKey, post_unit: UnitKey }
  | Mismatch { diverged: List<RuntimeIdentityDivergence>, pre_unit: UnitKey, post_unit: UnitKey }
  | Unprovable { why: List<RuntimeIdentityGap>, pre_unit: UnitKey, post_unit: UnitKey }

// THE MATCH FN. Total: no error type in its signature, nothing that can panic in its body (no indexing, no
// unwrapping, every match exhaustive over closed unions), and it takes ONLY the two sides -- the axis table
// is read from the catalog this module owns, so a caller cannot weaken the verdict by passing a friendlier
// policy.
//
// The order of the three checks IS the ruling:
//  1. Divergences first. Two readings that disagree is the strongest evidence available about the pair, and
//     it outranks any gap: a unit with a wrong serial is a Mismatch even if its model string never came
//     back. Silence never launders a contradiction.
//  2. Gaps next, and only over axes the table makes DECISIVE -- plus any corroborating gap is recorded but
//     cannot be fatal, so an unanswered corroborating axis leaves the verdict Match rather than dragging an
//     honest unit into Unprovable.
//  3. Match last, and only with the checked-and-passed axes listed.
fn runtime_identity_match(
  pre: PreBootIdentityStanding,
  post: PostBootIdentityReport,
) -> RuntimeIdentityVerdict {
  let examined = examinations(pre: pre, post: post)
  let diverged = all_divergences(examined: examined)
  let gaps = fatal_gaps(examined: examined)
  if diverged.length() > 0 {
    Mismatch { diverged: diverged, pre_unit: pre.unit, post_unit: post.unit }
  } else if gaps.length() > 0 {
    Unprovable { why: gaps, pre_unit: pre.unit, post_unit: post.unit }
  } else {
    Match { axes_matched: axes_checked_and_passed(examined: examined), pre_unit: pre.unit, post_unit: post.unit }
  }
}

// Every axis where BOTH sides read and the readings disagree, in table order. A flat_map over the table
// rather than a filter/map pair over a probe union: an axis with nothing to report contributes NO element,
// so there is no "unreachable" placeholder value anywhere in this module that a bug could surface as a
// verdict.
fn all_divergences(examined: List<AxisExamination>) -> List<RuntimeIdentityDivergence> {
  examined.flat_map(e => match e {
    AxisCompared { axis: a, pair: p, weight: _ } => if axes_agree(pair: p) { [] } else { [RuntimeIdentityDivergence { axis: a, readings: p }] }
    AxisUnanswered { axis: _, gaps: _, weight: _ } => []
  })
}

// The gaps that can void a verdict: a gap on either side of any DECISIVE axis. Corroborating gaps are
// deliberately not gathered here -- that is where "a mute BMC does not condemn a unit" lives.
// A gap voids a binding only when the axis that stayed quiet was DECISIVE, matched on the carried weight at
// the point of use rather than on an `is_decisive` bool: the arm that was matched stays visible in the code
// that acts on it, and a new weight makes this stop compiling instead of quietly returning false.
fn fatal_gaps(examined: List<AxisExamination>) -> List<RuntimeIdentityGap> {
  examined.flat_map(e => match e {
    AxisUnanswered { axis: _, gaps: gs, weight: w } => gs.flat_map(g => match w {
      DecisiveAxis => [g]
      CorroboratingAxis => []
    })
    AxisCompared { axis: _, pair: _, weight: _ } => []
  })
}

// The axes a Match may claim: decisive axes, read on both sides, compared and passed. A corroborating axis
// that agreed is not claimed, because it was not required -- claiming it would let a Match look stronger
// than the table says it is.
fn axes_checked_and_passed(examined: List<AxisExamination>) -> List<RuntimeIdentityAxis> {
  examined.flat_map(e => match e {
    AxisCompared { axis: a, pair: _, weight: w } => match w {
      DecisiveAxis => [a]
      CorroboratingAxis => []
    }
    AxisUnanswered { axis: _, gaps: _, weight: _ } => []
  })
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PER-AXIS READINGS AND COMPARISONS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// WHAT EACH AXIS SAYS ABOUT A PAIR, in ONE pass the fold shares. Two decisions the fold makes -- does
// anything diverge, and does anything stay unproven -- are derived from this single list, so they can never
// disagree about what the standings said. `axis_is_decisive`, `both_sides_read` and the per-row gap/divergence
// wrappers used to answer these as Bools derived separately, each re-deriving the standings and each free to
// disagree; the weight is carried here instead of being flattened to "is decisive".
fn examinations(pre: PreBootIdentityStanding, post: PostBootIdentityReport) -> List<AxisExamination> {
  runtime_identity_axis_rows.map(r => match r.axis {
    BoardSerialAxis => examine_axis(
      row: r, pre: pre.board_serial, post: post.board_serial,
      build: (pv, ov) => TextPair { pre_text: pv, post_text: ov })
    BiosVersionAxis => examine_axis(
      row: r, pre: pre.bios_version, post: post.bios_version,
      build: (pv, ov) => TextPair { pre_text: pv, post_text: ov })
    ModelAxis => examine_axis(
      row: r, pre: pre.model, post: post.model,
      build: (pv, ov) => TextPair { pre_text: pv, post_text: ov })
    NicSetAxis => nic_examination(row: r, pre: pre.nics, post: post.nics)
  })
}

// THE READINGS OF ONE AXIS ON BOTH SIDES.
//
// A SUM, not a record with four fields two of which are always placeholders. The two agreement rules
// consume different evidence -- an exact-equality axis has no addresses to compare, an address-set axis has
// no text to compare -- and one flat record would force every string axis to carry `[]` where addresses
// should be and every address axis to carry a rendering where a reading should be. A flat shape then lets
// one rule read the half it does not use: the divergence this shape makes impossible is a MAC axis
// "agreeing" because two rendered strings happened to match, or a serial axis comparing empty address lists
// and calling that a shared interface. Which arm a pair is follows from the axis, in `examine_axis`, the only
// place a pair is ever built -- so the rule that runs is decided by the evidence in hand and nothing else.
type AxisReadingPair
  = TextPair { pre_text: NonEmptyStr, post_text: NonEmptyStr }
  | AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: List<Eui48Address>, post_addresses: List<Eui48Address> }

// THE AGREEMENT RULES, indexed by READING KIND -- which is the only way this can be written so a
// misconfiguration cannot fabricate a verdict. There used to be an `AxisComparison` field on the row and a
// dispatch on both the row's policy and the pair's arm; that let a reviewer-visible edit to the table (give
// the address axis `ExactStringEquality`) produce a `Mismatch` for units whose addresses agree, reporting a
// divergence that never happened. Two independent facts that can contradict each other, and the code's
// answer to the contradiction was a fabricated verdict -- DESIGN section 5 forbids exactly that, and
// prefers the shape where the wrong state is unwritable over one that catches it.
//
// So the rule travels WITH the evidence: a text pair is compared by exact equality, an address pair by a
// shared permanent address. Which kind an axis produces is settled by `examine_axis`, the single place a
// pair is ever built, and no row can now overrule it. The two rules stay two rules because they are
// genuinely different claims -- the address rule is NOT exact equality, since a firmware reports every
// interface it can boot from while the booted OS reports only what it brought up, and on this fleet's boxes
// those sets legitimately differ (a management NIC the firmware lists and the OS leaves down). Exact
// equality there would make every real unit Unprovable; requiring nothing would let a different machine
// through.
//
// A future axis that needs a THIRD rule (a TPM quote compared by containment, say) adds an arm to
// `AxisReadingPair` and an arm here, and the compiler asks for both -- that is the cost this shape buys
// with, and it is cheaper than a table that can state a policy the fold cannot honour.
fn axes_agree(pair: AxisReadingPair) -> Bool {
  match pair {
    TextPair { pre_text: pt, post_text: pt2 } => pt == pt2
    // Both lists were screened non-empty by `examine_screened_addresses`, which is the only constructor
    // of this arm, so a shared-none result here means two real addresses that differ -- a divergence --
    // and an empty list can never reach it to masquerade as one.
    AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: pa, post_addresses: po } => mac_sets_share_an_address(pre: pa, post: po)
  }
}

// AN ADDRESS BINDS ONLY IF IT CAN NAME AN INTERFACE, and the row's binding authority is the predicate that
// says which addresses can. Octet equality alone is a silent widen (DESIGN section 5): a locally
// administered address is chosen by software and a group address names no single interface at all, so a
// bridge, a VM, or a renamed NIC could present one to any machine and be agreed with any other -- a Match
// from an address that belongs to nobody. Both sets are therefore filtered through the cited predicate
// BEFORE intersecting, which is what makes the row's Decisive weight earned rather than claimed. Filtering
// is intersection-only: it can turn an agreement into a non-agreement, never the reverse, and the fold
// reports that as a divergence an operator sees rather than as silence.
// THE NIC AXIS IS SCREENED BEFORE IT IS COMPARED, which is why it cannot use `examine_axis` like the
// three text axes: for a text axis, an answer is evidence, but for this axis an ANSWER CAN FAIL TO BE
// EVIDENCE, and the two cases have different verdicts.
//
// WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS, and what it used to be:
//  - both sides keep at least one permanent address and share one   -> AxisCompared, agrees  -> Match
//  - both sides keep at least one and share none                    -> AxisCompared, diverges -> Mismatch
//  - either side keeps NOTHING                                      -> a located gap on THAT side -> Unprovable
// The middle row is the only one that may call itself a divergence, because it is the only one where two
// pieces of evidence actually contradict each other. The third row used to fall through the comparison and
// come out as a Mismatch, which condemned a unit twice over: two sides that both answered 00:00:00:00:00:00
// (the null address, which the IEEE reserves and which is what a stack reports when it has NO address --
// `extdeps.network.mac.mac_address_is_null`) agreed, and two sides where the OS reported an empty list
// disagreed with a firmware that had plenty. Neither is a divergence; both mean "this axis did not answer",
// and now they say which side failed and how, so an operator can tell a mute NIC from a probe that read the
// wrong file. The screen is not re-implemented here: `mac_is_permanent_interface_identity` is the authority,
// and it excludes null, locally administered and group addresses together (merry-fox-82's side-chat verdict on d5a72591).
fn nic_examination(
  row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow,
  pre: AxisStanding<List<Eui48Address>>,
  post: AxisStanding<List<Eui48Address>>,
) -> AxisExamination {
  let report = standing_report(pre: pre, post: post)
  match report {
    BothAnswered { pre_value: pv, post_value: ov } =>
      examine_screened_addresses(
        row: row,
        pre_reported: pv.length(),
        post_reported: ov.length(),
        pre_eligible: pv.filter(a => mac_is_permanent_interface_identity(addr: a)),
        post_eligible: ov.filter(a => mac_is_permanent_interface_identity(addr: a)))
    PreQuiet { absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
    PostQuiet { absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
    BothQuiet { pre_absence: _, post_absence: _ } => unanswered(row: row, report: report)
  }
}

// THE FIVE OUTCOMES OF THE SCREENED PAIR, with the pre-side gap listed first so a both-sides-empty pair
// reports the firmware side first -- the same reading order the fold uses everywhere else.
// ONE GAP FOR A SIDE THAT ANSWERED WITH NON-EVIDENCE. Written once because all four combinations of
// (pre short, post short) must say the SAME thing about the side that failed, with the same counts and the
// same located side; four inline copies is the DESIGN section 3 fork, and one of them drifting would be a
// gap that reports the wrong count for the firmware side only.
fn no_eligible_gap(row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow, side: IdentitySide, reported: Int) -> RuntimeIdentityGap {
  RuntimeIdentityGap {
    axis: row.axis, side: side,
    absence: NoEligibleEvidence { reported_count: reported },
  }
}

fn examine_screened_addresses(
  row: RuntimeIdentityAxisRow,
  pre_reported: Int,
  post_reported: Int,
  pre_eligible: List<Eui48Address>,
  post_eligible: List<Eui48Address>,
) -> AxisExamination {
  // The gaps for the sides that came back with non-evidence, in firmware-then-OS order. Both are built
  // unconditionally through the one constructor so the two sides cannot drift in wording, counts or side.
  let pre_gap = no_eligible_gap(row: row, side: PreBootFirmwareSide, reported: pre_reported)
  let post_gap = no_eligible_gap(row: row, side: PostBootOsSide, reported: post_reported)
  let pre_short = pre_eligible.length() == 0
  let post_short = post_eligible.length() == 0
  if pre_short || post_short {
    let gaps =
      if pre_short && post_short { [pre_gap, post_gap] }
      else if pre_short { [pre_gap] }
      else { [post_gap] }
    AxisUnanswered { axis: row.axis, gaps: gaps, weight: row.weight }
  } else {
    // Both sides hold evidence, so whatever follows is a real answer about the machine. The pair carries
    // the SCREENED lists, because those are what the rule compared: reporting raw readings here would
    // show an operator addresses that were never part of the decision.
    AxisCompared {
      axis: row.axis,
      pair: AddressSetPair { pre_addresses: pre_eligible, post_addresses: post_eligible },
      weight: row.weight,
    }
  }
}

// PURE INTERSECTION, ON PURPOSE. The screen has ALREADY run -- `examine_screened_addresses` is the only
// constructor of the pair this reads and it filters through `mac_is_permanent_interface_identity` there --
// so filtering again here would be the same fact derived twice, which is what produced the unreachable arm
// review 81890 caught: two derivations of one thing, free to disagree. What remains is the one claim
// this axis makes: the two sides share at least one of the addresses they both hold as permanent.
fn mac_sets_share_an_address(pre: List<Eui48Address>, post: List<Eui48Address>) -> Bool {
  pre.any(a => post.any(b => same_mac_address(left: a, right: b)))
}

// Addresses compared octet by octet, so the comparison is about the address and not about how it was
// rendered -- render_mac_address is for reporting, never for deciding.
fn same_mac_address(left: Eui48Address, right: Eui48Address) -> Bool {
  left.octet1 == right.octet1
    && left.octet2 == right.octet2
    && left.octet3 == right.octet3
    && left.octet4 == right.octet4
    && left.octet5 == right.octet5
    && left.octet6 == right.octet6
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DECLARED CONSUMER FRONTIER (DESIGN 3c: name the consumer and its route, or classify).
//
// WHY A CARRIER RATHER THAN A COMMENT. Nothing outside this module's own witness imports it -- which is
// TRUE AND EXPECTED for a seam that lands ahead of the cut that reads it, but which a reader (or a
// reviewer, three rounds running) cannot distinguish from abandonment by looking. std.dissolution already
// models "an obligation discharged by a declaration that does not exist yet" as a bound trigger over a
// DeclarationRef whose status is COMPUTED against a declaration population. So the frontier is a row here,
// and its status is an answer rather than a claim: nothing in this comment says who has landed, because
// prose describing who-has-landed is a snapshot that goes stale within a day.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// THE CONSUMER THAT MUST EXIST: the S9 network-boot arm actuator, which boots a unit and whose whole
// purpose after that boot is answered by this module -- S10 verifies, against these readings, that the
// machine that answered on the network is the unit the attempt was made for. The name is not invented
// here: work item node://adhoc-9cc8b205-64b carries `network_boot_arm_actuator_steps` as its scope, which
// is the only thing that distinguishes a forward reference from a private fiction (a name nobody has agreed
// to write would report Pending forever and look like tracking). The ref is also written as a LITERAL in
// this module's witness, so the module cannot silently rename the frontier out from under the claim.
//
// WHAT A FIRED TRIGGER DOES NOT PROVE: that anything EXECUTES this seam. It proves the named declaration
// exists. A green witness over this row is evidence of a declared frontier, never of integration -- read
// Pending as "the consumer has not landed", and Fired as "it has been declared", nothing stronger.
data runtime_identity_match_consumer_frontier: DissolutionCondition = BoundDissolution {
  trigger: DeclarationAppears { ref: runtime_identity_match_consumer_ref() },
}

fn runtime_identity_match_consumer_ref() -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.provisioning.network_boot_actuation", decl_name: "network_boot_arm_actuator_steps")
}

// THE FRONTIER AS A MACHINE CAN ANSWER IT. The caller supplies the declaration population it actually read;
// there is no default, because "nothing is present" and "nobody looked" are different answers and a frontier
// that cannot tell them apart reports itself discharged.
fn runtime_identity_match_frontier_status(present_decls: List<DeclarationRef>) -> DissolutionStatus {
  dissolution_status(condition: runtime_identity_match_consumer_frontier, present_decls: present_decls)
}
